## Task

Decompose the approved design into an ORDERED list of
implementation tasks. Each task carries an `id`, a short `title`,
and a list of `acceptance_criteria` bullets. Emit as many tasks as
the design needs — there is no upper bound.

Acceptance criteria MUST reference skill-driven build/test verbs
when installed skills teach them. Fall back to the raw commands
only when no matching skill exists.

Test-coverage rule: every task that changes behavior MUST carry at
least one acceptance criterion that adds or extends an automated
test exercising that behavior (unit or integration, using the
project's existing test framework). Pure-refactor or docs-only
tasks may skip it, but must say so explicitly in the title or a
criterion.

Sizing rule (load-bearing): each task MUST fit ONE coding
sub-agent session. Target roughly ≤5 files touched and ≤6
acceptance criteria per task; split any larger unit of work (a
whole CR, a subsystem migration) into several tasks. A task that
cannot be completed in one session will be delivered partially —
prefer many small tasks over few large ones.

Ordering rule: tasks are applied strictly in array order by a
single implementation lane in one shared workspace. A task MAY
assume every earlier task has already been applied and build on
its changes; it MUST NOT depend on any later task.

Every entry is a real task — no placeholder/empty-title slots.
Set `num_tasks` to `len(tasks)`.

## Output

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loomv2/scripts/loom.sh
$LOOM output init "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task tasks-author
$LOOM output add  "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task tasks-author \
    --set 'tasks[].id=t1' \
    --set 'tasks[-1].title=<title>' \
    --set 'tasks[-1].acceptance_criteria[]=<criterion>' \
    --set num_tasks=1
```
